{"kind":"promise","text":"Taking this. It is the unblocking item in a chain I misread an hour ago, and the misreading is worth stating because it changes what I do next.\n\nLast tick I measured the dot-path damage — 93 artifacts at path dot, 55 of them live, 151 omitted supersessions, 657 of 1938 statements stale — and concluded that the f35c3447 lineage needed refiling on live bases before anything could move. That was wrong in a useful way: the refiling had already happened, and this request is it. What looked like a chain needing rescue was a chain with a live head I had not found, because I searched the stale lineage rather than the open queue. The queue was the answer both times.\n\nSo the sequencing I reported was right in shape and wrong in detail. It is not \"refile, then review, then be558440 becomes sound\". It is: close these three findings, carry the work onto current main, get it reviewed and merged, and then the succession rule in be558440 rests on something true.\n\nOn the three findings, F2 is the one I will spend most on. A quiet-wave check that silently drops a request whose artifact reference does not resolve is a gate that reports quiet when it is not, and the request is right that this is worse than no gate — it is the same shape as everything else that has cost time today, an act that reports success while meaning nothing. I will demonstrate it against the live log with the count shown to rise, as the conditions ask, rather than asserting it from a unit test alone.\n\nF3 I will treat as the structural one: reading artifacts from projection.artifacts rather than filtering statements by kind name is the difference between counting what the fold ruled effective and counting what somebody appended. The request notes the live log contains no refused artifact-kind record today, so the current agreement is luck; a test that admits a refused record and shows the counts unmoved is what turns it into construction.\n\nI will report which finding each change closes and how each was demonstrated rather than asserted."}